rStuff CENSUS -- PRE-REGISTRATION
Written 2026-08-22 BEFORE a single grep was run. Clay's dispatch amendment 1,
mark 2. Committed before counting so it cannot be rationalised afterwards.

WHAT IS BEING COUNTED
Every dereference through rStuff that is not dominated by a null check:
  C++ side   ->rStuff->
  rtn/twk    bare `rStuff.` and `field.rStuff` followed by a member read
Reported per site as: site / enclosing caller / can a specimen reach it.

CLAY'S PREDICTION, ON RECORD
"Low single digits, most unreachable by twins."

CLOD'S PREDICTION, ON RECORD, AND DELIBERATELY MORE SPECIFIC SO IT CAN FAIL
1. TOTAL unguarded sites: I predict MORE than "low single digits" on the raw
   grep -- I expect 10 to 30 raw hits -- but I expect the count that SURVIVES
   classification to be low single digits. Stated separately on purpose: an
   undercount reads as a smaller problem and an overcount as a bigger one
   (rule H9), and I want both numbers exposed.
2. REACHABLE BY A SPECIMEN: I predict 0 to 2 beyond the two already ruled
   (opDot case 36, runRuleAction).
3. WHERE THE UNREACHABLE ONES LIVE: I predict the bulk sit in the parse walk
   and rule-modification machinery -- parse(), testOptions, checkInput,
   setParse, modify -- reached only through a registered rule, which by Ruling
   A's producer-side assert (mark 3) will carry rStuff by construction.
4. CAN'T-TELL ROWS: I predict at least 1. If I report zero can't-tells I have
   probably defaulted somewhere rather than flagged, which is the failure this
   clause exists to catch.

⚠ THE ANTI-VACUITY CLAUSE, pre-registered because it is the one that matters.
A census is an instrument and inherits every rule on the list (H9). This one
has TWO known-positive rows -- opDot case 36 and runRuleAction -- and BOTH
MUST APPEAR IN THE RAW HITS. If either is missing from the grep output, the
grep is wrong and the whole count is void. They are the control pair, not
decoration.

⚠ AND THE CLASSIFICATION IS THE FINDING, NOT THE GREP. H9's corollary, paid
for twice on this project: read the hits by eye before reporting a number. A
sentinel census reported 1, then 6, then 4 for the same population.
